---
import '../styles/pkg-i.css'
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import PageShell from '../components/PageShell.astro'
import { breadcrumbTrail, getPage } from '../config/pages'
import { AUTHOR, GA_MEASUREMENT_ID, SITE } from '../config/site'
import { breadcrumbs, webPage } from '../seo/schema'
import { capitalise, numberWord } from '../data/format'
import { ENDPOINT } from '../analytics/track'

/**
 * The privacy page.
 *
 * The site asks one question, and only about one thing: Google Analytics
 * (TASK-039). It sets cookies, so under ePrivacy it needs consent, and it gets
 * none of it without: nothing from Google loads until the visitor accepts.
 * Everything else here needs no consent, because nothing else stores or reads
 * information on the visitor's device for a purpose they did not ask for. The
 * only other device storage is their own progress, written when they use the
 * app, which is the service they came for.
 *
 * What every part of it requires is telling people what happens, which is
 * this page. If it ever stops matching the code, that is a bug.
 */
const page = getPage('/privacy/')
const trail = breadcrumbTrail(page.path)

const schema = [
  webPage({ path: page.path, name: page.h1, description: page.description, aboutMorse: false }),
  breadcrumbs(trail),
]

/** GA's two cookies by name, so the page names the real ones. */
const gaCookies = GA_MEASUREMENT_ID ? ['_ga', `_ga_${GA_MEASUREMENT_ID.replace(/^G-/, '')}`] : []

/** The short version, as a ledger: what there is and is not, before the detail. */
const ledger = [
  { item: 'Cookies', value: 'Only if you accept GA', on: false },
  { item: 'Accounts', value: 'None', on: false },
  { item: 'Advertising', value: 'None', on: false },
  { item: 'Your progress', value: 'Your browser only', on: true },
  { item: 'Page views', value: 'Cloudflare, cookieless', on: true },
  { item: 'Usage counts', value: 'Our own, no identifier', on: true },
  { item: 'Google Analytics', value: 'Off until you accept', on: false },
]

/** What sits in your browser's local storage, one entry per stored record. */
const stored = [
  {
    name: 'Your progress',
    detail: 'which characters you have met and how you have done on each, per skill.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Your settings',
    detail: 'speed, pitch, key bindings, motion and whether the usage counter is on.',
  },
  { name: 'Your statistics', detail: 'sessions, streak, XP, achievements and best scores.' },
  { name: 'Your daily challenge results', detail: 'the last few weeks of them.' },
  { name: 'Your theme', detail: 'dark, light or following your system.' },
  {
    name: 'Your answer about Google Analytics',
    detail:
      'allowed or not, and when you chose. It is left out of backups, and erasing your data keeps it, so neither one brings the question back or carries a yes somewhere you did not give it.',
  },
]
---

<BaseLayout title={page.title} description={page.description} path={page.path} schema={schema}>
  <PageShell width="prose" trail={trail} eyebrow="Station notice" h1={page.h1} intro={page.intro}>
    <dl class="mf-instrument grid overflow-hidden px-3 py-2 sm:px-4">
      {
        ledger.map((row) => (
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 border-b border-line px-4 py-3 last:border-b-0">
            <dt class="mf-engraved">{row.item}</dt>
            <dd class="flex items-center gap-2.5 text-right font-mono text-control text-ink">
              {row.value}
              <span
                class="mf-lamp"
                data-tone={row.on ? 'amber' : undefined}
                data-on={row.on ? 'true' : 'false'}
                aria-hidden="true"
              />
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <div class="mf-prose pi-manual mt-12">
      <p>
        This site asks you one question, once: whether you allow Google Analytics. It stays off
        until you answer, and nothing from Google is loaded before then. Everything else on this
        page needs no permission, because none of it stores or reads anything on your device for a
        purpose you did not ask for: your progress and settings are the service itself, and neither
        of our own two counters sets a cookie or keeps an identifier on your device.
      </p>

      <section>
        <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
        <p>
          {capitalise(numberWord(stored.length))} things, in your browser's local storage. None of them
          are sent to any server, and none of them are readable by anyone but you.
        </p>
        <ul>
          {
            stored.map((entry) => (
              <li>
                <strong>{entry.name}</strong>: {entry.detail}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p>
          Clearing your browser data erases all of it, and there is no copy anywhere else to restore
          from. If it matters to you, export it from{' '}
          <a href="/settings/">settings</a> — it is a plain JSON file you keep.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What is counted</h2>
        <p>
          Three things. The first two are counts rather than records of a person, and run without
          asking. The third runs only if you accept it.
        </p>

        <h3>Our own usage counter</h3>
        <p>
          When you start a practice session or finish a game, the browser sends a request to{' '}
          <code>{ENDPOINT}</code> on this site, naming that event: <code>practice_start</code>, and
          sometimes a short label like <code>listening</code>. That is the whole message. There is
          no identifier, no session token, nothing you typed, and no way to connect two events to
          the same person.
        </p>
        <p>
          The server answers with an empty response and stores nothing; the request line in its own
          access log is the only record, and a scheduled script turns those lines into daily totals.
          You can turn this counter off in <a href="/settings/">settings</a>, and it is never sent
          at all if your browser asks not to be tracked. That switch and that signal cover this
          counter only, not the Cloudflare beacon below; Google Analytics has its own switch.
        </p>

        <h3>Cloudflare Web Analytics</h3>
        <p>
          A small script from Cloudflare counts page views. It reports the address of the page you
          are on, the page you came from, your browser's user agent and how quickly the page loaded.
          It is{' '}
          <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/" rel="noopener"
            >cookieless by design</a
          > and does not fingerprint you or follow you between sites. Cloudflare processes this on our
          behalf. It does not depend on your answer about Google Analytics, because it sets nothing on
          your device.
        </p>

        <h3 id="google-analytics">Google Analytics, only if you accept</h3>
        <p>
          Until you choose, a bar at the bottom of the page asks. Nothing from Google is loaded and
          no Google cookie is set while it waits, and none if you reject. If your browser sends
          Global Privacy Control or Do Not Track, that counts as a no and you are not asked at all.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you accept, Google Analytics 4 records the pages you view (their address without
          anything after a <code>#</code>), the page you came from, your browser, device type,
          screen size and language, your approximate location (country and city, worked out from
          your IP address, which Google Analytics does not store), and how you move through the
          site: how long a visit lasts, scrolling, and links you follow out of it. It sets two
          first-party cookies, {
            gaCookies.map((name, index) => (
              <>
                {index > 0 && ' and '}
                <code>{name}</code>
              </>
            ))
          }, holding a random identifier so that visits from the same browser count as one visitor.
          They expire 13 months after your last visit.
        </p>
        <p>
          None of it is used for advertising. The advertising consent signals are always sent as
          denied, and Google signals and ad personalisation are switched off in the code, so no
          advertising profile is built from your visit. Google keeps the event-level data for 2
          months and then deletes it; only aggregated totals outlive that. Google processes it on
          our behalf, and it may be processed in the United States.
        </p>
        <p>
          To withdraw, open <a href="/settings/#data">settings</a> and set Google Analytics to Not allowed.
          That stops it on the page you are on, deletes its cookies from this browser, and it does not
          load again. Your answer is kept in this browser only, so another browser, or one whose data
          you have cleared, asks again.
        </p>
        <p>
          Neither Google Analytics nor the Cloudflare beacon is loaded on the{' '}
          <a href="/message/">secret message</a> page, and neither is the bar, because the message lives
          in that page's address and must not be sent anywhere.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Your IP address</h2>
        <p>
          Like every web server, ours records the address each request came from. That is
          unavoidable — it is how a reply finds its way back — and the log is what makes it possible
          to see that a site is being attacked. If you allow Google Analytics, Google's servers see
          it too, for the same reason, and use it only to estimate where you are.
        </p>
        <p>
          The script that produces our own visitor counts does not keep addresses. It hashes each
          one with a secret that is regenerated every day and never stored, counts the hash, and
          discards it. Two visits on the same day collapse into one count; the same person tomorrow
          does not, so nobody can be followed across days — not even by us. The raw logs rotate
          daily and only the most recent few days exist on the server.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What there is none of</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            No cookies unless you accept Google Analytics, and then only its two. Not even a
            "necessary" one otherwise.
          </li>
          <li>No accounts, no email addresses, no names.</li>
          <li>No advertising, no advertising networks, no data sold or shared for advertising.</li>
          <li>
            No fonts and no images from anyone else's server — they are all served from this domain.
            Two scripts are not, both described above: Cloudflare's analytics beacon, and Google's
            analytics script once you have accepted it.
          </li>
          <li>No profiling for advertising and no automated decisions about you.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          You can check all of this rather than take our word for it: open your browser's developer
          tools on any page here and look at the cookie jar and the network tab. The{' '}
          <a href={SITE.repo} rel="noopener">whole source is public</a>, including the script that
          processes the logs and the one that asks about Google Analytics.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Your rights</h2>
        <p>
          Under the GDPR you can ask what data is held about you, ask for it to be corrected or
          deleted, object to how it is processed, and withdraw consent at any time, as easily as you
          gave it. In practice there is very little to ask about: we hold no account, no contact
          details and nothing that identifies you. Google Analytics data is tied to a random cookie,
          not to your name, and its event-level data is deleted after 2 months. Your progress is
          already in your hands — deleting it is a button in <a href="/settings/">settings</a> and needs
          nobody's permission.
        </p>
        <p>
          For anything else, or to ask a question about this page, contact{' '}
          <a href={AUTHOR.url} rel="me">{AUTHOR.name}</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-12 border-t border-line pt-6 text-sm text-muted">
      If this page ever stops matching what the site does, that is a bug. Please{' '}
      <a href={`${SITE.repo}/issues`} rel="noopener" class="pi-link">report it</a>.
    </p>
  </PageShell>
</BaseLayout>
